\section{Setting up the diagnostic quiz, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the quizzes of \cref{prior-knowledge,end-measure}: it sets
them up in the learning platform, and it prepares their results for the
analysis.
Three files are tangled from this section: [[quiz-start.json]] and
[[quiz-end.json]], the descriptions of the start-of-course and
end-of-course quizzes that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to Canvas, and
[[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches the results from Canvas, summarises
them, and prepares the open-text answers for coding.

\subsection{The two quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-end.json
\end{minted}
There are two quizzes because the data collection is chronological: the
start-of-course quiz asks the background items and the knowledge part
(\cref{prior-knowledge}), and the end-of-course quiz repeats the
knowledge part to measure the gain (\cref{end-measure}).
The two descriptions reuse the same chunks, so the knowledge items are
defined once and pushed twice.

<<[[quiz-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Diagnostic quiz: your programming background",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz maps your programming background so that we can adapt the course. It is not graded and there are no wrong answers. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    <<common quiz settings>>
  },
  "items": [
    <<background items>>
    <<knowledge-part items>>
  ]
}
@

The end-of-course quiz opens with a question of its own: whether the
student prepared for the quiz.
We ask because preparation would confound the knowledge-gain measure of
\cref{end-measure}---a student who reviews the material the night
before measures preparation, not what the course left behind.

<<[[quiz-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Concept quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the concept questions from the start-of-course quiz, so that we can see what you have learned during the course. It is not graded. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    <<common quiz settings>>
  },
  "items": [
    <<end-quiz preparation item>>
    <<knowledge-part items>>
  ]
}
@

Both quizzes are diagnostics, not assessments: nothing is graded, so
there is no time limit, a single attempt suffices, and the result view
shows the student their answers but no notion of correctness.
These settings are shared; only the title and the instructions above
differ.

